#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199077} :activity/id #uuid "da7c19c9-a092-5227-8c3d-c60c670a8222" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048206 :topic/id #uuid "8850e3a0-f2fb-5671-843a-13422a22aa79" :topic/math-academy-id 4387} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199560 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $A$ and $B$ are sets, the Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs $(a,b)$ whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\big\\{(a, b) \\: : \\: a \\in A,\\, b \\in B \\big\\}\n$$\n\nMany Cartesian products have natural representations in the Cartesian plane. The most common of these is the Cartesian product $\\mathbb R^2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbb R^2 = \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R =  \\big\\{(x, y) \\: : \\: x,y \\in \\mathbb R \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis infinite set contains all ordered pairs $(x,y),$ where $x$ and $y$ can be any real numbers. In other words, $\\mathbb R^2$ contains every point in the Cartesian plane! \n\n![](images/07/070389fcdb6d69f9aa90dabbd98a0d3461e26d3e3e08b1d53b92e99688c09923.png)\n\nSimilarly, the set\n\n$$\n\\mathbb R^3 = \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R =  \\big\\{(x, y, z) \\: : \\: x,y,z \\in \\mathbb R \\big\\}\n$$\n\ndescribes every point in three-dimensional Cartesian space. \n\n![](images/e7/e74fb5948144bc61173e4de73865cb746278bd31fd87e4abf927fa01821ba936.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c18e6618-573b-5560-8d95-58ed56f05155" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8237 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c7652427-e5ff-5d02-9f23-39039f090aff" :step/math-academy-id 153383 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198272 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198169 :question/id #uuid "bb40c174-3739-50df-835e-5d47c9902d5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13192"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421830}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "2190f1df-5853-5d8c-85e2-33f40f1f0f24" :step/math-academy-id 153384 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199579 :tutorial/content "Cartesian coordinates can also be used to visualize other Cartesian products.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following Cartesian product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^- = \\big\\{(x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in\\mathbb N,\\, y\\in\\mathbb R^-\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis product contains every point in $\\mathbb R^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative real number.\n\nTo visualize this Cartesian product, consider the following subset of our Cartesian product:\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\big\\{ (1,y) \\: : \\: y \\in\\mathbb{R}^- \\big\\} \\subset \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-\n$$\n\nThis subset describes all points in the plane whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative real number. We can represent $S_1$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f6165f66b4149b7b2ea52a3cd686836853d85048ca890e850cb771174a150c57.png)\n\nNext, we consider the subset $S_2,$ given by\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\big\\{ (2,y) \\: : \\: y \\in\\mathbb{R}^- \\big\\} \\subset \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-.\n$$\n\nThis subset describes all points in the plane whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative real number. \n\n![](images/ca/caf2299a2e8009ad5b2776ce67786fa7c7e3bdb57aacbd7df87882f59a2350e2.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $S_3, S_4, S_5,\\ldots,$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N\\times {R}^{-} & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup ⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-$ as the set of vertical rays shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/033c40b86bfd5cebf24d34de644f632213207386c2b67c559af7594468599e1e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1626e562-8e88-5ff3-bae3-4162438c0086" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8329 :tutorial/title "Cartesian Products of Countably Infinite Sets"} :step/id #uuid "cd371945-c79b-5b78-9017-15c39e4d5ed7" :step/math-academy-id 153387 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198154 :question/id #uuid "eae94514-cd92-5405-90b1-ee09dad18e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-13162"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050088}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389916}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "48b1fb46-7071-5897-9dd7-a47e1d955f6c" :step/math-academy-id 153388 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199584 :tutorial/content "Let's sketch a representation of the set $S= [0,1] \\times [0,1]$ in the Cartesian plane.\n\nThe Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS = [0,1] \\times [0, 1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS=\n\\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in [0,1],\\, y\\in [0,1] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_0 \\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}0}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS_{\\color{red}0} = \\big\\{ ({\\color{red}{0}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in [0,1]\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_{\\color{red}0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/1c/1cc30fcc7543bfbbf8c5c59d1436eb3b1a0799d60c9ad1d899957e8fd17d58ab.png)\n\nNow, consider a general $S_{\\color{blue}{i}} \\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some ${\\color{blue}{i}} \\in [0,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS_{\\color{blue}{i}} = \\big\\{ ({\\color{blue}{i}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in [0,1]\\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor each ${\\color{blue}{i}} \\in [0,1],$ $S_{\\color{blue}{i}}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $({\\color{blue}{i}},0)$ to the point $({\\color{blue}{i}},1).$\n\n![](images/28/28b9aa0559635ed4090f10dd0b59f2cbb6c297f8736d9509a637b5d78d27561c.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I = [0,1]{:}$\n\n$$\nS = \\bigcup_{{\\color{blue}i} \\in I} S_{\\color{blue}i}.\n$$\n\nAs $\\color{blue}i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union $\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{{\\color{blue}i} \\in I} S_{\\color{blue}i}{:}$\n\n![](images/76/76ff48755dbfc3ba1943665d08aa8cfd00948bbc1ddfaab560a0af64f11bae26.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "923fb377-d694-5181-8561-c57612f15054" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8339 :tutorial/title "The Cartesian Product [0,1] x [0,1]"} :step/id #uuid "ef01039c-9cce-558a-8067-2ffba087e848" :step/math-academy-id 153392 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198205 :question/id #uuid "aa337681-5d8c-5ea1-b0fe-5d8cd15040a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13320"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390333}] :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "9ddfd97d-7a1a-59ef-8492-3fbe4088f22f" :step/math-academy-id 153393 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198275 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198206 :question/id #uuid "67569060-2f33-5a8d-ae73-690742d3d318" :question/math-academy-id "e-13321"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389797}] :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "5777ec1b-f050-570f-86e4-1e7bffa77dfb" :step/math-academy-id 153396}] :activity/title "Visualizing Cartesian Products" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198532}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184960} :activity/id #uuid "2d8d094d-3193-5fac-913b-86ed4139db30" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048482 :topic/id #uuid "a35b2494-edfb-5f14-91d5-3c70b0096e14" :topic/math-academy-id 3771} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184960 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185298 :tutorial/content "The $n$th-degree Taylor polynomial of a function $f(x)$ around the point $x=a$ is\n\n$$\nP_n(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\dfrac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 + \\cdots + \\dfrac{f^{(n)}(a)}{n!}(x-a)^n.\n$$\n\nIn particular, the coefficient of the $k$th-degree term is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8570bb84-4036-5426-9b9a-4050bb0a0f6b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1289 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "813e9605-20b7-56b3-aa91-7c8f9b64c19b" :step/math-academy-id 2535 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184961}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184961 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184189 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184087 :question/id #uuid "05dec044-4b3b-5f42-88c5-b7ab323b0e97" :question/math-academy-id "e-743"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22d992a1-2ea8-5226-9971-188447f98ab6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050866}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416141}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Higher-Degree Taylor Polynomial Of a Given Function About a Given Point"} :step/id #uuid "2af6d490-9670-5c6f-871c-b37af160c40d" :step/math-academy-id 2536 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184962}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184962 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184190 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184088 :question/id #uuid "38bd1eff-19ac-5922-aa68-f9c30c9f5ea3" :question/math-academy-id "e-744"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a832b1a2-e80e-53d0-bb64-03df0f1a209c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413289}] :knowledge-point/title "Obtaining a Higher-Order Derivative Of a Function Given its Higher-Degree Taylor Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "0fe424cb-a6ec-5b72-809e-e18f76d90bda" :step/math-academy-id 2538}] :activity/title "Higher-Degree Taylor Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186184370}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079296} :activity/id #uuid "b20c9f5a-b450-5954-8b2f-bbe839892fde" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048563 :topic/id #uuid "aa3e68fe-4581-52ac-895f-4225e07db905" :topic/math-academy-id 632} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079296 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079704 :tutorial/content "In addition to the properties that we've already discussed, there are several more properties that we can use to calculate definite integrals.\n\nThe first property states that if the lower and upper limits of a definite integral are the same, then the integral evaluates to zero: \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\color{blue}a}^{\\color{blue}a} f(x) \\, \\textrm d x = 0.\n$$\n\nFor example, in the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\color{blue}3}^{\\color{blue}3} x \\, \\textrm d x ,\n$$\n\n the upper and lower limits of integration are the same. If we evaluate the integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{3}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere's an intuitive explanation for this result. The integral $\\displaystyle\\int_3^3 x \\, \\textrm d x$ can be thought of as the area under the graph of $y = x$ between $x=3$ and $x=3$, which is a rectangle of zero width. A rectangle of zero width has no area!" :tutorial/id #uuid "26d18176-2077-5153-8ab9-e60785e25e5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 779 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "86e79d43-4ad8-54e9-9f94-9f653c6a1746" :step/math-academy-id 1492 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079297}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079297 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078425 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078290 :question/id #uuid "c02d2000-3fef-59e4-9127-84500ef83eb8" :question/math-academy-id "e-418"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a2c6bf6-41f2-5f8d-8799-5664ba941dc8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050057}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079032} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319037} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364323}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Over an Interval of Length Zero"} :step/id #uuid "141c8f26-4787-55fe-ac88-f25213bbe1e0" :step/math-academy-id 1494 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079298}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079705 :tutorial/content "The second property states that reversing the limits of integration changes the sign of the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\color{red}a}^{\\color{blue}b} f(x)\\, \\textrm d x = - \\int_{\\color{blue}b}^{\\color{red}a} f(x)\\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nSo, for example, if we know that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\color{red}1}^{\\color{blue}2} 2x\\, \\textrm d x =3,\n$$\n\n \n\nthen we can immediately conclude that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{\\color{blue}2}^{\\color{red}1} 2x\\, \\textrm d x = -3.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e62a5fc4-17c9-5afb-bfea-b3d72535936e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 782 :tutorial/title "Reversing the Limits of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "76a21cf7-c7f3-58ba-b746-c866d1d0e6be" :step/math-academy-id 1497 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079299}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079299 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078426 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078291 :question/id #uuid "c7a90d29-e8b9-5a06-94f7-776eb254cc52" :question/math-academy-id "e-419"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff2f1a9d-2b0e-5a18-98d5-4f49f6f1527b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364090} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385309}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Integral Given Another Integral With Reversed Limits of Integration"} :step/id #uuid "86f99e5a-7017-53e2-99de-68e1d35694ab" :step/math-academy-id 1498 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079300}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079706 :tutorial/content "The last property states that if we have the sum of two integrals with the same integrand, and where the lower bound of one integral matches the upper bound of the other integral, such as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^{\\color{blue}0} 4x^2\\, \\textrm d x + \\int_{\\color{blue}0}^{5} 4x^2\\, \\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nthen we can combine the two integrals into a single integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-3}^{\\color{blue}0} 4x^2\\, \\textrm d x + \\int_{\\color{blue}0}^{5} 4x^2\\, \\textrm d x = \\int_{-3}^{5} 4x^2\\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\int_a^c f(x) \\, \\textrm d x + \\int_c^b f(x) \\, \\textrm d x = \\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo understand the reasoning behind this rule, remember that the integrals represent area. So, the rule states that the area bounded by a function on the interval $[a,b]$ is equal to the sum of areas on the adjacent intervals $[a,c]$ and $[c,b].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0dbaffac-ad36-5df8-898b-ef366cd640ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 784 :tutorial/title "The Integral of a Function Over Adjacent Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "e72a483a-204e-5b98-9edc-e094d1b7d15c" :step/math-academy-id 1500 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078427 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078295 :question/id #uuid "a85f51c5-601b-52ec-82dd-0b611842d3d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-420"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9693b76e-ba77-5314-bfbf-508b25bb1072" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416140}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Given Integrals Over Adjacent Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "aa3bf8cd-0e62-5b48-a131-0fc027187804" :step/math-academy-id 1501 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078428 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078304 :question/id #uuid "3d925b89-dc4d-59e0-bf00-3a3ca90b5169" :question/math-academy-id "e-421"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d752aac-6187-595d-8818-b0179fd7380e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319087} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362853}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Definite Integral Using Multiple Properties of Definite Integrals"} :step/id #uuid "05a7e09c-1b45-5d1a-adf8-02c9162f1406" :step/math-academy-id 1503}] :activity/title "Properties of Definite Integrals Involving the Limits of Integration" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078656}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106897} :activity/id #uuid "f9fd061e-b62c-59b3-95e7-cb70aa81601f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049254 :topic/id #uuid "e6f9ad03-abe7-52d6-8a6a-acbcb8fbeef7" :topic/math-academy-id 1351} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106897 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107154 :tutorial/content "Consider the triangle below with vertices $(0,0),$ $(2,1)$ and $(2,3).$\n\n![](images/f7/f7c16deb083b467c063c900196274d08c532ad4633354f375530cb2cbe270491.png)\n\nWhat is the image of this triangle under the action of the transformation  $\\mathbf{T}$ represented by the matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all vertices of our triangle written as columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(0,0),$ $(2,0),$ $(2,0).$ Notice that these points lie on the same line. Two of them even coincide!\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the following line segment:\n\n![](images/88/88383b8eca1805c158f80d998d844d4c666b15d118a0ec26b4a1ba43c781fc76.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\nThis is an example of a linear transformation that does not preserve linear independence. \n\n* First, notice that since [math]\\det(T)= 0,[/math] the matrix [math]T[/math] is singular, which means that the vectors represented by the columns of [math] T,[/math] given below, are *not* linearly independent. \n\\[\n\\left\\{ \\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix}, \\:  \\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix} \\right\\}\n\\]  \n\n- Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ and region $D,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = |\\det(T)| \\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\n \nSince $|\\det(T)|  = 0$ in this case, any object applied to $\\mathbf T$ collapses to an object with zero area.\n\n- Also, the fact that $|\\det(T)|  = 0$ tells us that $\\mathbf T$ has no inverse transformation. Therefore, it is impossible to recover the original triangle knowing only its image and the matrix $T.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "260cef8c-3f93-55cc-ac8c-258b2555991e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7693 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "91fe4680-2271-5bd9-a679-84fcae2b774c" :step/math-academy-id 44641 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106898}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105460 :question/id #uuid "dcd503a8-a9c6-524b-a8ac-0641ce3e1107" :question/math-academy-id "e-12423"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049420}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360722}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "1608691b-a3f7-54f4-82a1-b60b84369109" :step/math-academy-id 44642 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105894 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105461 :question/id #uuid "964efb4a-6e6b-5fc1-a993-20aa2b0acdaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-12424"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395215}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "cdca196d-b54e-5e0f-8148-f89ac24552f6" :step/math-academy-id 44645 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106901}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105895 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105462 :question/id #uuid "4aa087bf-ee6c-5e90-9431-c4be6b207333" :question/math-academy-id "e-12438"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281413}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "fe3d3d75-3555-5f42-96ec-0bc13603e728" :step/math-academy-id 44693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107155 :tutorial/content "Given a linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with an associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ there are three possibilities regarding the type of object formed under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ by a two-dimensional preimage. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the three-sided polygon $\\mathcal P$ with vertices $(0,1)$, $(2,0)$ and $(2,1),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/97/97689c7aaf7f6d42314fd01cb36b557df8081880659bcea08905e6c57f358ac1.png)\n\nLet's describe the three possible types of object formed by the image of $\\mathcal P{:}$\n\n- Case where $T$ is non-singular:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that $T$ is non-singular by computing its determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $\\mathcal P$ and then compute the product $TX{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a triangle.\n\n![](images/14/145753e4f69dfa67bb45f59b7e9ed8bca789c589d4c5340ce2112363fa70cd7b.png)\n\n- Case where $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular and has at least one nonzero entry:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that $T$ is singular by computing its determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of  $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment.\n\n![](images/46/46d387dbf6678d324135dbbb3439688c0bdbf9f9567baef56f34aa4aa9ee22e3.png)\n\n- Case where $T$ is the zero matrix:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation represented by the zero matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nClearly, $T$ is singular. Now, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are all $(0,0)$, the origin $O$ of the coordinate system. \n\n![](images/db/dbb9fce92da1f231a31e110f6cabb6faaf1bb43961a6c1de012c7c302c7af928.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "0bc95d9b-74ad-509c-8c28-e4e34606b376" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7702 :tutorial/title "Image Types Formed by Linear Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "c766a184-7dd4-51fc-8815-53eaecbc38c4" :step/math-academy-id 44695 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107156 :tutorial/content "In general, for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system." :tutorial/id #uuid "593c9d77-3990-5aef-8f2f-1ef87dd41c59" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7708 :tutorial/title "A Summary of Image Types"} :step/id #uuid "b9b4bf14-bd02-5562-869e-62d28d1f3854" :step/math-academy-id 44720 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106900}}] :activity/title "Singular Linear Transformations in the Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105986}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097624} :activity/id #uuid "8e49429d-91d6-54f6-a697-b46fb59d6f57" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049983 :topic/id #uuid "23d28f28-6e03-59a9-9aed-cdda7589cb10" :topic/math-academy-id 1087} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097800 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the sequence $a_n,$ given by\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac {n^3 + 5 n}{2 n^3 - n^2}, \\qquad\\ n\\geq 1,\n$$\n\nand suppose that we want to determine the **limit of the sequence** $a_n.$ In the context of sequences, this means to work out $\\displaystyle\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} a_n.$\n\nFinding the limit of a rational sequence $a_n$ is very similar to finding the horizontal asymptotes of a rational function. We divide the numerator and the denominator by the variable part of the dominant term in the denominator. \n\nIn our case, the variable part of the dominant term in the denominator is $n^3,$ so we divide the numerator and denominator by $n^3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}+5n}{2{n}^{3}-{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}+\\frac{5n}{{n}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}}-\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(1+\\frac{5}{{n}^{2}})}{(2-\\frac{1}{n})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the finite limit exists, we say that the sequence **converges** and its limit is $\\dfrac 1 2.$\n\nIf the limit of a sequence does *not* exist, then we say that the sequence **diverges**." :tutorial/id #uuid "67373759-4b5b-5913-9a45-f853199756d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1145 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "90a35c01-4f8c-59a7-8680-a8d6b6bc08dc" :step/math-academy-id 2253 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096740 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096548 :question/id #uuid "fc06df19-d6de-5730-a239-fe02cc42cd59" :question/math-academy-id "e-643"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d14dbef-9a16-537e-a182-87fd6853e543" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097351} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416142}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Divergent Sequences"} :step/id #uuid "1b60c9c7-cc54-584f-aab8-346823bb8e39" :step/math-academy-id 2254 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097628}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096741 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096549 :question/id #uuid "0615e448-c18c-5f47-aa1b-18f0045cbbea" :question/math-academy-id "e-644"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bec57d9-3abf-58cb-87cb-1449224e6c2e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319216}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Limit of a Convergent Rational Sequence"} :step/id #uuid "3ae3906f-5738-53e0-9b1e-44c8419f4ccd" :step/math-academy-id 2257 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097625}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096550 :question/id #uuid "5de361e2-c574-556d-80ef-e7d75ffa93fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-645"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46630d98-f27d-5426-aaad-0c390078e9b9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400100}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of a Factored Rational Sequence"} :step/id #uuid "b98531af-a699-57e5-91c9-c7b47c22850f" :step/math-academy-id 2260}] :activity/title "Limits of Sequences" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096876}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069669} :activity/id #uuid "28988cb0-4f45-5ead-9f9b-e977ea21e629" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050117 :topic/id #uuid "2e70a953-3c7e-56c2-ab7b-ae151e69a396" :topic/math-academy-id 427} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069974 :tutorial/content "The long division method allows us to divide a polynomial by another nonlinear polynomial.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-4x^3+4x^2+x+4}{4x^2-1}\n$$\n\nHere, the divisor is the quadratic polynomial $4x^2-1$. \n\nAgain, we follow a step-by-step process.\n\n**Step 1**\n\nWe start by writing the dividend and the divisor as follows:\n\n![](images/30/30699e4c70a9787e299ddd72cf874be4d745d7765307118e244c7140900f9a8c.svg)\n\n**Step 2**\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}{-4x^3}})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{\\color{red}{-4x^3}}{{\\color{blue}{4x^2}}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{-x}}.$ We add this result to the quotient line:\n![](images/4d/4dc1f1597f96ce624bb5848694836fb7913119c5c5a771b8bc5cf2d5ce918a7f.svg)\n\nNext, we multiply the $\\color{#8b008b}-x$ we just found by the full divisor ${\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}-x}({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1)=-4x^3+x.$ We place this underneath the dividend, being sure to match the terms. Notice that we include a zero coefficient for the $x^2$ term.\n![](images/93/935c739a119cdb202ccf0ac919266888f79f2b002025a047df1350f85e8a0f14.svg)\n\nNow, we subtract $-4x^3+0x^2+x$ from $-4x^3+4x^2+x+4$ and get $4x^2+0x+4\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/3d/3d60d3f2969615aab7ee5d3fa32b95846558f6b2329fa530524b7ee5af53c8aa.svg)\n\n**Step 3**\n\nWe now repeat the process. The new dividend is ${\\color{red}{4x^2}}+0x+4.$\n\nWe divide the leading term of the dividend $({\\color{red}{4x^2}})$ by the leading term of the divisor $({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}).$ This gives $\\left(\\dfrac{{\\color{red}{4x^2}}}{{\\color{blue}{4x^2}}}\\right)={\\color{#8b008b}{1}}.$ We  add this result to the quotient:\n![](images/7b/7bc4e0d178e61af627a4e3c27e74f4dac447148144fcbd195bd064afe540c418.svg)\n\nNext, we multiply the ${\\color{#8b008b}{1}}$ we just found by the full divisor ${\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1.$ This gives ${\\color{#8b008b}{1}}({\\color{blue}{4x^2}}-1)=4x^2-1.$ We place this result underneath the dividend:\n\n![](images/bf/bf35c387201a18236997a8e0687093fc51f9281b03374e864fd36176402dbbd8.svg)\n\nFinally, we subtract $4x^2+0x-1$ from $4x^2+0x+4$ to get ${\\color{red}{5}}\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/68/68c9581aedc9c0bb2d061869483ddce11fe27579577d2a4d68c02e7c4cc9fc46.svg)\n\nWe're left with the constant $\\color{red}5$, which has a smaller degree than the divisor. So, the division is complete.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n- the quotient of the polynomial division is ${\\color{blue}{-x+1}},$ and\n\n- the remainder is ${\\color{red}{5}}.$\n\nOur complete result can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{-4x^3+4x^2+x+4}{4x^2-1}={\\color{blue}{-x+1}} + \\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{4x^2-1}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "65b9f3fa-d1aa-5799-bc53-d2a843778980" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3382 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "44a9504c-2712-520b-9afa-31a3bf6cea61" :step/math-academy-id 18896 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069670}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068761 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068599 :question/id #uuid "a2aac357-23c5-5038-84c6-bc263bd45afa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5143"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da0ca17e-50f3-5f67-bb1c-27868f7b4f45" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049759}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323966}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Cubic Polynomial by a Quadratic Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "9f200fd9-719c-597f-b531-42d1b3346597" :step/math-academy-id 18897 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069750}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068762 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068600 :question/id #uuid "bd9a160c-574e-506e-865c-d08061c75aed" :question/math-academy-id "e-5144"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "525be9c7-af11-506b-be69-444dfc640add" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323968} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326675}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Polynomials When the Dividend Contains Zero Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "b176dca5-6b75-5e46-ada1-b11bd84387bd" :step/math-academy-id 18898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068817 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068660 :question/id #uuid "9438b41b-2ad4-5248-881c-cb938b6cf1c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-8932"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "76a7314b-4ebf-52eb-b464-97865ea9a81e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241088} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416139}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Quartic Polynomial by a Quadratic or Cubic Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "6438e377-6aac-5674-a6de-e4d8421e658a" :step/math-academy-id 33336 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069671}}] :activity/title "Dividing Polynomials Using Long Division" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068960}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092992} :activity/id #uuid "eedb9663-ba7e-5ff2-ad61-597482d1636e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050944 :topic/id #uuid "73f31c1a-1d34-559e-8292-a932c5c83421" :topic/math-academy-id 934} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093402 :tutorial/content "If the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ is invertible, then we can find a solution to the system \n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nby \"pre-multiplying\" both sides of the equation by $A^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ {I}_{2}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the solution of $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b},$ we simply calculate $\\mathbf{x}=A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor instance, to find the solution to the system of equations\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n we first calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the solution to this system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solution is $x=3$ and $y=2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "39b17ee8-086a-5282-a414-c88d455b6107" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3049 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7bdb49ae-e07e-5e55-b869-25f67ee0ac21" :step/math-academy-id 16467 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092993}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092183 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092023 :question/id #uuid "cb7d8dc0-98eb-53d0-9e48-3b1c2d7f6308" :question/math-academy-id "e-4508"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c89778fa-3c8d-5f0a-a0f8-c9b3d9193ffd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050825}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394786}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "7e787e4f-33b0-514d-ad4f-569e9dfb525d" :step/math-academy-id 16468 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092994}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092184 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092024 :question/id #uuid "422d31ad-8caa-5ce2-a0e0-c905fcc2640d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4509"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4691976a-d825-5f6c-aed7-0d9b45560952" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393215}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 System of Equations Using an Inverse Matrix With Reorganization"} :step/id #uuid "696d1030-ccb5-5376-b23e-62d38e346070" :step/math-academy-id 16469 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092998}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093404 :tutorial/content "Consider the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=3 \\\\ 6x+3y=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCan we use the matrix method of $\\mathbf{x} = A^{-1}\\mathbf{b}$ to find a solution to this system?\n\nThis system can be represented in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo use this matrix method, we need to find $A^{-1}.$ However\n\n$$\n\\det (A) = 2\\cdot 3 - 1\\cdot 6 =0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is not invertible. So we cannot use the matrix method to find a solution to the system.\n\nIn general, if the coefficient matrix $A$ is not invertible, there is no unique solution. This means that either\n\n- a solution may not exist, or\n\n- there may be infinitely many solutions." :tutorial/id #uuid "e5e2bff7-df5b-5628-92e3-a50ca8dd6be8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3066 :tutorial/title "Systems of Equations Where the Coefficient Matrix is Singular"} :step/id #uuid "ff58cfed-22a0-501b-8983-7edc00d85964" :step/math-academy-id 16614}] :activity/title "Solving 2x2 Systems of Equations Using Inverse Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092433}]}
